Let's look at the expansion above:
\[\frac{1}{2} + \frac{-2}{4} + \frac{3}{8} (\text{which is equal to} \frac{4}{8} -\frac{2}{4} =\frac{1}{8}) +\dots\]
<answer>
We'll denote this partial sum as $S_n$;
\begin{align*}
S_n &=
\frac{1}{2} - \frac{2}{4} + \frac{3}{8} - \frac{4}{16} + \frac{5}{32} - \dots + \frac{n}{2^n} (-1)^{n + 1}
\\ &=
(\frac{1}{2} - \frac{4}{8}) + \frac{3}{8} (-1 + 1) + \frac{5}{32} - \dots + \frac{n}{2^n} (-1)^{n + 1}
\\ &=
\frac{-3}{8} + \frac{3}{8} + \frac{5}{32} - \dots + \frac{n}{2^n} (-1)^{n + 1}
\\ &= \frac{1}{2} - \frac{3}{8} - \dots + \frac{n}{2^n} (-1)^{n + 1}
\\ \end{align*}
<answer>
This is a partial sum of the series with terms $\frac{n}{2^n} (-1)^{n + 1};~n=1,2,3,\dots$, so now we see that
\begin{align*}
\lim_{n \to \infty} S_n &=
\lim_{n \to \infty} (\frac{1}{2} + \frac{-2}{4} + \frac{3}{8} + \frac{-4}{16} + \frac{5}{32} + \cdots + \frac{n}{2^n} (-1)^{n + 1})\\ &=
\lim_{n \to \infty} \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \frac{n}{2^n} (-1)^{n + 1}
\\ &= \frac{4}{9}
\end{align*}
So the sum is $S = \frac{4}{9}.$
</answer>
